---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import InteriorHero from '../../components/InteriorHero.astro';
import SectionShell from '../../components/SectionShell.astro';

/**
 * Step 30 narrowed this page. Garages moved to
 * `/services/garage-floor-coating-edmonton`, which now owns that topic outright;
 * what is left here is every other residential room A8 names — barn, workshop,
 * kitchen, bathroom — plus the basement rooms the homepage talks about. The two
 * pages link to each other and do not repeat each other's arguments.
 */
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Residential epoxy flooring',
  serviceType: 'Residential epoxy and decorative floor coating',
  provider: { '@type': 'HomeAndConstructionBusiness', name: 'Final Finish Epoxy', telephone: '+1-780-991-1651', email: 'finalfinishepoxy@gmail.com' },
  areaServed: { '@type': 'City', name: 'Edmonton' },
  description: 'Decorative epoxy, flake, and metallic floor coatings for Edmonton barns, workshops, basements, kitchens, and bathrooms.',
};
---
<BaseLayout title="Residential Epoxy Flooring Edmonton | Final Finish" description="Decorative epoxy, flake, and metallic floors for Edmonton barns, workshops, basements, kitchens, and bathrooms. How to choose, and what preparation involves." canonicalPath="/services/residential-epoxy-flooring">
  <script slot="head" is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main-content">
    <InteriorHero
      eyebrow="Barns · Workshops · Basements · Kitchens"
      title="The rooms in the house nobody finished."
      lead="Decorative epoxy, flake, and metallic coatings for the concrete rooms you already own. Looking for a garage floor? That has its own page."
    />

    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">What it is</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">A finish chosen around the room, not a catalogue.</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Residential work covers barns, workshops, basements, kitchens, and bathrooms. Three finish families are available: decorative epoxy, flake, and metallic.</p>
      <p>What changes between rooms is not the chemistry. It is the light, the traffic, and what the space has to hold. A barn floor and a basement floor want different things from the same three materials.</p>
      <p>For a garage, start at the <a href="/services/garage-floor-coating-edmonton">garage floor coating page</a> instead. It covers vehicles, salt, and storage in detail.</p>
    </SectionShell>

    <SectionShell tone="stone">
      <p class="section-kicker">When each finish is the right choice</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Pick for the room, then look at colour.</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="editorial-card">
          <h3>Flake</h3>
          <p>A patterned field that hides everyday grit well. Good in a workshop or a barn, where the floor takes real use. See it against your own cabinets and lighting before you commit; the <a href="/resources/floor-flake-colour-design-guide">flake and colour guide</a> sets out a sample process.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="editorial-card">
          <h3>Decorative epoxy</h3>
          <p>A calmer, more even surface. It suits a kitchen, a bathroom, or a basement that reads as a room rather than a shop. Ask how the proposed texture handles wet feet and the cleaning method you actually use.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="editorial-card">
          <h3>Metallic</h3>
          <p>A poured composition rather than a repeating pattern. Every floor lands differently, so look at samples and agree where the effect stops. The <a href="/resources/metallic-epoxy-expectations">metallic expectations guide</a> covers what to ask.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>The <a href="/finishes">finish library</a> shows all three families, and the <a href="/floor-visualizer">floor visualizer</a> puts them on a photo of your own room.</p>
    </SectionShell>

    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">What preparation involves</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">The slab decides more than the colour does.</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Grinding comes first. It opens the concrete so the coating bonds, and it is the same step in every room we work in.</p>
      <p>Cosmetic crack filling follows. Interior rooms bring their own questions: an old coating under the lino, a patch where a wall used to be, a low spot that pools when you mop. Mention water history early. A coating is not a fix for water coming through a slab, and finding that out after the grind helps nobody.</p>
      <p>Photos are enough to start the conversation. The <a href="/resources/floor-coating-site-assessment-checklist">site-assessment checklist</a> lists what to record, and <a href="/services/concrete-solutions">concrete solutions</a> goes into repair and levelling.</p>
    </SectionShell>

    <SectionShell tone="stone">
      <p class="section-kicker">What to expect</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Two days, give or take the concrete.</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Installation usually runs two days, and it varies with the slab. An interior room also has to be emptied, and grinding is loud and dusty.</p>
      <p>Talk through access before the date is set. A basement means carrying equipment down stairs, and a kitchen means the room is out of use while the work is on. Both are easier to plan for than to find out on the day.</p>
    </SectionShell>

    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">What we do not promise here</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Ask for the specifics in writing.</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">No price, no return-to-use time, and no coverage term appears on this page. Those belong to your floor, and they are written into your quote.</p>
      <p>Two more things this page will not claim. A coating is not slip-proof, and the right texture depends on footwear, water, and how you clean. A coating also does not replace moisture work, ventilation, or heating; it is one part of a room. Bring the <a href="/resources/questions-to-ask-floor-coating-contractor">contractor question list</a> to any quote, including ours.</p>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/contact#quote">Send photos for an estimate</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/finishes">Look at the finishes</a>
      </div>
    </SectionShell>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
